            <ArticleTitle>Cutting and stenting in a heavily calcified left anterior descending artery lesion.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>547-51</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The significance of heavily calcified proximal left anterior descending coronary artery stenosis in a 57-year-old man was assessed physiologically using a Doppler flow wire. Intravascular ultrasound guidance allowed for adequate dilatation with a cutting balloon and optimization of stent deployment. The cutting balloon offers an effective alternative in this challenging scenario.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
